WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.929 --> 00:00:03.929
Paul Joseph Gulino, Associate Professor at
Chapman University Dodge College of Film and

00:00:03.929 --> 00:00:10.570
Media Arts: Okay, so here’s an interesting
problem that you see with scripts that maybe

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:18.650
don’t work that well, they have talk but
no dialogue.

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:30.520
Talk, human speech can be poetic, it can be
witty, clever, polite, moving but it isn’t

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:37.920
dialogue unless there is action under it and
that’s something to look for.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:44.080
There’s really two elements with dialogue
that you can learn about.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:46.610
It isn’t simply a talent.

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:50.059
Some people have a natural talent, they can
have an ear for it and capture it.

00:00:50.059 --> 00:00:51.060
But it goes beyond that.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:59.650
You can actually learn how to do this and
by action…well let me explain it this way.

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:05.890
In a dramatic scene you will often find…not
a dramatic scene, in a scene the problem is

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:10.460
no drama, the students will come with what
I call “Q&amp;A dialogue”

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.290
“How are you today?”

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:14.030
“Okay, how about you?”

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:15.110
“Well not bad.

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:16.790
I think I’ll go to the store.

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:17.790
What about you?”

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:22.159
“Well I think I’ll not go to the store.”

00:01:22.159 --> 00:01:26.230
That’s Q&amp;A dialogue, it doesn’t really
qualify as dialogue.

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:33.280
It’s useful to think of dialogue (especially
in the rewrite stage and I’d emphasize that)

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:36.970
in the first draft just pour it on the page
and don’t worry if it’s Q&amp;A dialogue,

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:38.299
don’t worry about it.

00:01:38.299 --> 00:01:40.990
It will only block you up if you worry about
it.

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:45.850
But once it’s on the page and if it’s
working and everybody loves it you say “We’re

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:46.850
done.”

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:52.020
If it isn’t working there are some diagnostic
tools to study your dialogue to see what you

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.270
can do with it.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:02:00.961
There is this concept of subtext in dialogue
and I’ll have to give you a warning I use

00:02:00.961 --> 00:02:04.049
the term different from pretty much everybody
else.

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:09.940
I’ve seen that news like in the [Robert]
McKee book where he talks about subtext and

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:15.269
dialogue and there’s a passage in there
where he says lets say you have two lovers

00:02:15.269 --> 00:02:21.860
and they are at a table and it’s beautiful
and candlelit and one says to the other “Darling

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:23.310
I love you, I love you.

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:24.310
I love you.”

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:30.370
Well that’s not actable because it’s too
on-the-nose (on-the-nose dialogue).

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:34.760
Well there really needs to be subtext and
it would be better if you give them action

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:43.359
and give the example of a couple that is repairing
a flat tire and we get from that action that

00:02:43.359 --> 00:02:46.689
they are in love but they don’t ever say
it.

00:02:46.689 --> 00:02:52.849
I mean I look at that and I think I feel that
I’ve been in that situation where I’ve

00:02:52.849 --> 00:02:57.540
been with someone and said I loved them and
it was actable because I did it.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:02:59.220
Why isn’t it actable?

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:05.089
Well that version of subtext I’ve never
found very helpful.

00:03:05.089 --> 00:03:10.060
I get the idea and you want to say things
without saying it otherwise it’s too on-the-nose.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:15.000
But I think it’s more useful to think of
subtext as the underlying format.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.559
What am I doing?

00:03:17.559 --> 00:03:19.099
What am I doing in the scene?

00:03:19.099 --> 00:03:22.450
An actor is asked “What am I doing?”

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:25.660
Dialogue is action with words, that’s all
it is.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:30.609
It’s somebody doing something but they’re
using words instead of actions, physical actions.

00:03:30.609 --> 00:03:35.290
So it’s a useful thing when you have a scene
that is already written to just see whether

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:38.790
you can reduce each line of dialogue to a
verb.

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:46.510
And the kind of verbs you’re looking for
are things like attack, defend, deflect, persuade,

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:47.760
seduce.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.430
Those are the kinds of verbs you want the
lines to have.

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:56.729
You don’t want to explain (it’s just emotionally
neutral) and that’s where the Q&amp;A thing

00:03:56.729 --> 00:03:57.729
comes in.

00:03:57.729 --> 00:04:02.530
So if you have the dialogue “How are you
today?”

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:05.629
And the person says “Well, I didn’t sleep
much last night.”

00:04:05.629 --> 00:04:06.829
That’s Q&amp;A.

00:04:06.829 --> 00:04:10.689
But if you have a character who says “You
look like hell?”

00:04:10.689 --> 00:04:12.939
And they say “Well I didn’t sleep much
last night.”

00:04:12.939 --> 00:04:14.479
You see now you’ve got attack/defense.

00:04:14.479 --> 00:04:16.199
“I didn’t sleep much!”

00:04:16.199 --> 00:04:20.269
So you transform it into something that actually
moves.

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:24.360
That actually is dynamic and we’re following
action.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:29.729
And that’s the other thing, if you want
to know what people…what’s the next screenplay

00:04:29.729 --> 00:04:34.270
that works, that gets people going, one other
element is that people follow action which

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:37.919
is somebody doing something or something doing
something.

00:04:37.919 --> 00:04:42.800
And even in your description you keep that
in mind that in a stage play you’re going

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:47.889
to list all of that stuff that’s on the
stage but in a movie you want a character

00:04:47.889 --> 00:04:56.110
to walk through a room and pass by a fancy
couch with a cat on it and move to a pick

00:04:56.110 --> 00:05:01.470
up a…you want it to be all contained in
action because people watch that.

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:03.740
So that’s subtext.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:06.930
I think it’s a useful way of thinking of
subtext, what’s the action?

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:09.159
What’s the verb that is underlying it?

00:05:09.159 --> 00:05:16.000
And you can stray a little bit from that but
most of the time you want to be able to identify

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.000
with that.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:18.479
What is the person doing?

00:05:18.479 --> 00:05:23.039
The question of on-the-nose dialogue is not
related to subtext.

00:05:23.039 --> 00:05:30.810
On-the-nose-dialogue is defined as characters
say exactly what they are thinking and it’s

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:32.220
straightforward and it’s boring.

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:37.620
So in that example okay man says to the woman
“I love you, I love you.”

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:41.159
Okay, that’s a bit on the nose.

00:05:41.159 --> 00:05:47.000
But the way to attack that again is in the
rewrite stage not when you’re trying to

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:48.599
come up with it.

00:05:48.599 --> 00:05:55.169
There are simple literary devices that are
ancient that you can employ and the easiest

00:05:55.169 --> 00:05:57.590
one in irony.

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:02.580
You take a character who says “You look
like hell.”

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:04.159
Okay that’s straightforward attack.

00:06:04.159 --> 00:06:06.840
And says “You look beautiful.”

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:09.200
Just flip it completely upside down.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:10.580
And they’ll be like “What do you mean?

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:11.639
I couldn’t sleep last night!”

00:06:11.639 --> 00:06:14.009
You know they’ll defend themselves because
they’re going to get it.

00:06:14.009 --> 00:06:21.639
Because a lot of human interaction is ironic
and we talked about it in the other book The

00:06:21.639 --> 00:06:27.990
Science of Screenwriting there are theories
that human beings in order to function as

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:30.999
a society need to have plausible deniability.

00:06:30.999 --> 00:06:37.139
You need to test things out like an explosive
issue like love relationships and dating.

00:06:37.139 --> 00:06:44.389
What if you’re interested in somebody but
you’re not sure if you should ask them out

00:06:44.389 --> 00:06:49.449
because maybe there’s a problem so you could
go up and tell them a story.

00:06:49.449 --> 00:06:54.409
“There was this guy I know who was in love
with this girl but she was already involved

00:06:54.409 --> 00:06:55.560
with somebody.”

00:06:55.560 --> 00:07:00.110
And the other (the target of the seduction)
may know exactly that they are talking about

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:04.970
her but if the person says “I’m married.”

00:07:04.970 --> 00:07:06.720
Well I wasn’t saying anything.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:10.509
I was telling a story, see?

00:07:10.509 --> 00:07:12.330
So you have irony.

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:21.110
Irony is basically saying two opposite things
at the same time like “It’s pouring rain.

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:22.819
I love this weather!”

00:07:22.819 --> 00:07:24.650
And people get it.

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:28.770
Or “Nice shoes you’ve got there.”

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:31.349
And people go “What wrong with them?”

00:07:31.349 --> 00:07:32.550
They’re going to understand it.

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:34.020
It’s in the tone and in the way that you
do it.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:35.080
But it’s like “I didn’t say anything.

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:36.229
I just said those are nice shoes.

00:07:36.229 --> 00:07:37.250
That’s all.”

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:46.180
So irony is a useful one and then there’s
TROUBLE IN PARADISE again (a great movie that

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:47.819
everyone should watch).

00:07:47.819 --> 00:07:53.610
But there’s a scene where there is two guys
that are rivals for the same woman and one

00:07:53.610 --> 00:08:00.710
of them goofed up and what did he…oh he
lost something that belonged to her on a date

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:02.980
and the other guy is riding them.

00:08:02.980 --> 00:08:04.719
So what do they do?

00:08:04.719 --> 00:08:11.999
They meet each other in a shop where you can
buy another one of what she lost (a purse).

00:08:11.999 --> 00:08:15.070
And the guy comes in and says “Oh, how are
you doing Major?”

00:08:15.070 --> 00:08:18.870
And then he says “You’re looking fine
Major.”

00:08:18.870 --> 00:08:23.249
And then he says “I’ve had enough of your
insulting remarks.”

00:08:23.249 --> 00:08:28.310
And it works very well.

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:30.630
So irony is one.

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:36.800
Then another one is metaphor that you’ll
see people speaking and people do this a lot.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:42.709
So instead of subtext I think a preferred
term would be indirection (it’s indirect

00:08:42.709 --> 00:08:45.480
speech) or sometimes you could call it conflicted
speech.

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:53.269
A speech that is rich because it has double
meanings and a great example that I like from

00:08:53.269 --> 00:09:00.029
the DOUBLE INDEMNITY picture, it’s a very
famous scene in which a man is interested

00:09:00.029 --> 00:09:01.529
in a married woman.

00:09:01.529 --> 00:09:09.420
And all he says when she says “My husband
won’t be back at 8 o’clock.”

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:14.010
He implies “I don’t want to see you anymore.”

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:17.180
And she says “There’s a speed limit in
this state, Mr. Neff.

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:18.940
45 miles an hour.”

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:21.630
And he says “How fast was I going officer?”

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:22.910
She says “I’d say about 90.”

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:26.430
He says “Why don’t you get off of your
motorcycle and give me a ticket.”

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:29.459
“Suppose I let you off with a warning this
time.”

00:09:29.459 --> 00:09:36.250
The whole thing is carried on indirectly through
metaphor and that’s a powerful way of doing

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:37.250
it.

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:38.260
Plus he can say “Hey, I was just talking
about traffic.

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:40.110
I was talking about a speeding ticket.

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:42.190
I wasn’t going after your wife, it’s fine.”

00:09:42.190 --> 00:09:47.940
So that’s part of the reason you have it
but also having it in your script makes it

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:51.949
richer, makes it like “Wow, this is really
pop and powerful.”

00:09:51.949 --> 00:09:55.870
That’s the other thing to keep in mind about
a screenplay as opposed to a novel is it’s

00:09:55.870 --> 00:09:58.830
a limited amount of time so it has to be richer.

00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:05.110
Everything they say in a novel is gossip,
drama is scandal.

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:06.910
Instead of walking, you’re running.

00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:10.579
So if you have dialogue, it’s precious and
you want it to be rich.

00:10:10.579 --> 00:10:12.860
So indirection can help.

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:23.253
Another one is…by the way THE SOCIAL NETWORK
is a great study and he uses irony in it an

00:10:23.253 --> 00:10:28.589
awful lot with a character saying “I’m
heartbroken about that.”

00:10:28.589 --> 00:10:30.940
You know that he is just fooling them.

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:36.399
Or somebody saying “This is really classy.”

00:10:36.399 --> 00:10:37.630
But said indirectly.

00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:40.290
“Let me let the classiness waft over me.”

00:10:40.290 --> 00:10:44.010
One character says and it’s clear his meaning
is not that.

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:49.880
Or another one on a cease and desist letter
“I guess that cease and desist letter really

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:51.050
scared the stuff out of him.”

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:53.530
It means it didn’t have any effect.

00:10:53.530 --> 00:10:59.920
All of what Sorkin did was flip them into
irony but he also uses metaphor.

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:03.959
“Well if these two characters want to stand
on my shoulders and pretend they’re tall,

00:11:03.959 --> 00:11:04.959
that’s fine.”

00:11:04.959 --> 00:11:10.880
Well they’re big, they’re not going to
stand on their shoulders.”

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:12.350
But he is using metaphor.

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:17.709
Metonymy (something stands for something else),
again going back to THE SOCIAL NETWORK.

00:11:17.709 --> 00:11:27.020
He tells the story about the founder of Victoria’s
Secret who sold out too soon.

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:31.050
He could have made 500 million, he only made
5 million.

00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:33.139
He threw himself off of the Golden Gate bridge,
right?

00:11:33.139 --> 00:11:34.810
That’s the story that’s told.

00:11:34.810 --> 00:11:40.320
And then he tells the Mark Zuckerberg character
“You know, you don’t want to sell out

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:43.300
too soon because the waters of the Golden
Gate are very cold.

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:49.750
He doesn’t have to say that you’re going
to regret this.

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:52.950
That’s on-the-nose dialogue.

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:59.180
If you say “The waters are cold” that’s
a much more way of doing it.

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:03.750
And metonymy is standing for something, the
class of things.

00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:08.210
The bridge stands for harming one’s self
or depression or failure.

00:12:08.210 --> 00:12:12.470
That’s what you can do to make dialogue
not on-the-nose.

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:20.320
And I would say subtext you want in every
line if you can.

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:25.600
In direction is more like spice (you add it
as needed) and look for opportunities but

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:29.709
people don’t use it all the time so you
don’t need to use it all the time.

00:12:29.709 --> 00:12:33.709
Film Courage: Well even in SHARP OBJECTS where
Amy Adam’s character goes back to the little

00:12:33.709 --> 00:12:37.690
town where she was raised and she doesn’t
really want to be there she says in the beginning

00:12:37.690 --> 00:12:39.990
of the show when they say “Bless Your Heart.”

00:12:39.990 --> 00:12:43.020
That doesn’t mean what it means (it basically
implies the opposite).

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:47.790
That’s what so great about the show too
is a lot of stuff is caged in this sweetness

00:12:47.790 --> 00:12:49.030
but doesn’t really mean it.

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:55.710
In fact I think it’s with Adora (the fantastic
character played by Patricia Clarkson) who

00:12:55.710 --> 00:13:02.440
is the mother and is so skilled at that kind
of dialogue where stuff is said to take the

00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:06.070
power away from someone and it’s done so
well.

00:13:06.070 --> 00:13:13.329
Paul: Yes, well there’s a movie I saw recently
again (going back a little further) called

00:13:13.329 --> 00:13:17.230
A BAD DAY AT BLACK ROCK with Spencer Tracy.

00:13:17.230 --> 00:13:24.270
And it’s not a Western, it’s set in Arizona
but it’s not a Western.

00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:30.170
It involves a guy showing up in this small
town where nobody has been…the train has

00:13:30.170 --> 00:13:33.540
not stopped in the last three years and it
suddenly let’s off this guy.

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:35.500
It usually goes straight on through.

00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:40.120
Nobody comes to Black Rock anymore and everybody
is acting real suspicious.

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:44.300
“Who is this guy and what does he want?”

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:48.389
And he’s trying to get information to go
find somebody and the moment they mention

00:13:48.389 --> 00:13:52.120
the name it’s like nobody wants to help
him.

00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:58.440
So the dialogue is very functional, the indirect
dialogue is very functional because he can’t

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:00.860
say “Okay, did somebody hurt them?

00:14:00.860 --> 00:14:01.860
What happened?

00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:02.860
Did somebody hurt him?

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:03.899
What’s going on here?”

00:14:03.899 --> 00:14:07.360
He’s got to ask questions and the don’t
want to give him a lot of answers.

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:13.700
So it’s all this verbal maneuvering and
it’s a sight to behold how they pulled that

00:14:13.700 --> 00:14:14.700
off.

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:15.700
It’s worth it.

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:16.700
It’s an exciting movie.

00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:20.019
It’s also worth looking at just for the
way indirection was used.

00:14:20.019 --> 00:14:25.550
Asking questions without asking them, answering
them, giving warnings to people without warning

00:14:25.550 --> 00:14:26.550
them.

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:31.800
That kind of thing, so it makes for rich screenwriting.

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:40.480
That would be another element if the dialogue
has power to it (power in the sense of action

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:46.300
and it’s not all on-the-nose and uses indirection
and subtleties the way humans do talk.

00:14:46.300 --> 00:14:50.759
Then it’s going to be more successful and
that’s it.

00:14:50.759 --> 00:14:53.660
It just takes practice and it just takes rewriting.

00:14:53.660 --> 00:14:56.509
It’s not that hard to do actually.

00:14:56.509 --> 00:15:00.029
Especially irony, just look at your stuff
and see what happens if the character says

00:15:00.029 --> 00:15:02.660
the opposite and find out.

